Good morning, church. Good to see all of you this morning. I want to welcome all of you.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.620
If we haven't met yet, my name is Pete. I'm one of the pastors here, and I get to share

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:17.600
God's Word with us this morning. If you want to open up your Bibles to Ephesians chapter

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.100
one, that's where we're going to be looking into today. As Bo was mentioning earlier,

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:25.260
we're kind of just starting this process of going through the book of Ephesians. It's

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:29.120
been an exciting season for our church as we just get kind of kicked off with a new ministry

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.160
year. New life groups have started. A lot of new people are coming and checking out

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:36.580
our church. I do want to welcome those of you who are visiting us today. Really glad you're here.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.480
Hope you get to meet God and see some of what he's doing in our church family this morning.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.920
And I hope all of you already have, as we were just discussing with each other,

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:48.440
just a lot of ways that you are being blessed and growing closer to the Lord. I think one thing I

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.640
have to share that really encourages me and has been a blessing to me is just seeing people

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.740
actually like really pursuing God. I've been so encouraged by that. And whether that's in like

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:01.780
just after our Sunday celebration, seeing people stick around a little longer just to pray

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:06.120
and seek God, even seeing people like praying for each other, ministering to each other,

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.080
just here, or even people coming up to receive prayer from our prayer team. And just hearing,

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.940
like as I meet up with people, hearing people saying like, yeah, I'm reading this in the scripture

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:20.340
these days, and God's speaking to me this way. It's just been so encouraging to be part of a

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:24.580
spiritual family where it's not just about kind of us living in community together, but us as a

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:28.320
community really seeking God together. So turn to someone next to you real quick and say, it's good

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:34.940
to seek God together? All right. And I think this is just a great way for us to be starting

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:40.200
this year of ministry, and especially as we shared about our annual theme of higher calling

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:45.380
and really wanting to rise above temporary pursuits and live for God's eternal purposes,

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:50.980
just seeing many of us saying, I want a closer relationship with God. And I think as we seek

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:54.740
that closer relationship and fellowship with him, well, other things will come out of that,

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:58.080
Like we've been talking about a life of faith or even a life of ministry.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:01.940
That comes out of the relationship with God that we're living in.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:06.460
And so we're going to really be digging deep into that, especially here in Ephesians chapter 1.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.880
And we'll keep going in that throughout the year.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.860
But I'm excited to get into that this morning.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:16.560
And for us to really dig into and think about what does it look like to not just know the gospel,

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.520
but then live in the truth of the gospel together.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:23.760
And I think that's something that's really powerful that can really change things in

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.240
our lives and in the world around us.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.660
And actually to get started, just with kind of a quick illustration of that, I wonder how

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:33.440
many of you here, if you want to, you can raise your hand, you don't have to, but how many

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.780
of you are familiar with this historical period called the Troubles in Northern Ireland?

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.680
Okay, there's like a couple of you that know what that is.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:45.180
Okay, it's all right, but some of you, when I describe it, you'll maybe be like, oh, okay,

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:46.180
yeah, I've heard of that before.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:58.980
There is this period in Northern Ireland known as the Troubles, and this started around the 1960s, and it started with this conflict about what the future of Northern Ireland would be.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:10.880
And it's something that really kind of took over that whole country for about 30 years, where there was this ongoing conflict, and it was a conflict between two parties, two groups of people.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:16.760
One of those were the nationalists or the republicans who were mostly Catholic, and

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:20.460
these were people who wanted a united Ireland, who wanted like Ireland and Northern Ireland

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:21.800
to be in unity together.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:25.780
And the other party were the unionists or the loyalists.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:30.500
These were mostly Protestant, and these are people who wanted Northern Ireland to remain

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:31.980
part of the United Kingdom.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:35.820
And so there was this whole conflict over this, and it wasn't just like a political

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.580
conflict, like politicians shaking their fingers at each other.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:46.000
it erupted into urban warfare, where paramilitary groups like the Irish Republican Army or the

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.780
Ulster Volunteer Force, you know, there were just consistent attacks, car bombings, shootings,

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:56.640
all these kinds of things happening over that 30-year period with different spikes, and then

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:01.020
like kind of a lull, and then another spike. But it was a consistent period of violence for three

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:07.100
decades. Over 3,600 people were killed during the Troubles, and estimates of between 30,000 and

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:12.640
50,000 additional injuries during that time. So this was not a small thing. This was something

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:18.720
that really took over this whole nation. And in that time, neighborhoods became segregated.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:25.340
And they were segregated along those party lines, where if people that were part of these different

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:31.120
parties wouldn't live in the same neighborhood with each other, they kind of blocked off these

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:35.700
neighborhoods with what they called peace lines. But actually, what a peace line meant is if you

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:39.460
cross it, there's not going to be any peace. Like, you're going to get attacked. You might get killed

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:45.300
if you go into the wrong neighborhood. So it was an incredibly divisive time in Northern Ireland's

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:50.820
history, and political solutions didn't seem to be going anywhere. Over these decades, politicians

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:55.860
tried to solve these problems. They tried to start negotiations, but anything that they tried

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:00.940
just stalled out, collapsed, because everyone's just so suspicious of each other. Nobody trusts

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:06.200
each other, to be true to their word. And so no solutions really went anywhere. And during these

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:10.220
times, I mentioned that one of these parties was predominantly Protestant and the other

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:17.960
predominantly Catholic. It wasn't necessarily a religious conflict, but it was more of a

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:24.000
political or a social conflict, and yet they kind of broke down into those groups. And so even a lot

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:29.420
of the institutional churches, Protestant and Catholic churches, would kind of buy into that

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:35.340
tribalism of their surrounding society and sort of pick sides and kind of see the others as enemies

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:40.600
and all that kind of stuff was going on as well. But at the same time, there were believers in

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:45.000
Christ in that country who wanted to live differently, who wanted to see a different

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:49.740
solution. So for instance, there was one group called the Cori Mila community that actually

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:55.020
became a space where Protestants and Catholics from warring neighborhoods would get together,

00:05:55.020 --> 00:06:01.200
they would live, they would eat, they would pray, they would dialogue together and really find unity

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.960
through faith in Christ. And this was something, a community that started to bring some healing

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:12.720
to the nation. Along with that, many clergy people at that time, on both sides, Catholic and Protestant,

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:19.440
would kind of act behind the scenes to open lines of communication and negotiation between these

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:24.300
warring factions. And so they would talk with opposing politicians, they would talk with these

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:30.380
opposing paramilitary groups and try to kind of bring people together as trusted intermediaries

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:36.040
in those conflicts. So the clergy kind of stepped up to try to play those roles. And most powerfully,

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:42.640
most substantially, there were many followers of Jesus at that time who chose to publicly forgive

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:47.800
those who had murdered their family members. Came out and publicly said that. And in doing so,

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:54.020
became a powerful witness of the gospel to bring reconciliation and restoration of those

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:59.400
relationships and in those communities. And the fruit of these believers living out their faith

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:04.920
and demonstrating the gospel in this way led to actually real change in this country. That

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:10.240
culminated specifically in what was called the Good Friday Agreement that was made on April 10th in

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:17.040
1998. This was kind of a huge turning point in this conflict, that after 30 years of violence and

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:22.540
bitterness and division and hatred, that finally these parties came together and said, we're going

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:27.420
to make peace, and we're going to put an end to this violence. And then there were still some

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:32.620
things that happened after that, but that largely brought about the end of this period known as

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:37.720
the Troubles. Now, I share that today partly because I think it's just a great testimony of the power

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:43.480
of the gospel to bring real change in the lives of people and even in communities. But at the same

00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:47.620
time, as we think about that, and I want us to know there is that power, we also recognize that

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:54.630
in a fallen world, these kind of political agreements come and go. It doesn't mean that

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:58.930
there's no more issues, that it will never flare up again, or that everything is totally solved.

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:04.410
But today, as we look into our passage in Ephesians 1, we're going to see actually a greater

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:10.270
and more ultimate hope and unity empowered by the gospel message. And we're going to explore

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:15.530
the power of the gospel to deal with the problem of sin in our lives and ultimately unite all

00:08:15.530 --> 00:08:18.690
things in Christ. That's what we're going to talk about today. I'm going to go ahead and give you

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:22.050
our one thing as we start out here today. A lot of times I save it at the end, but I'll give it to

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:27.910
you at the beginning. It's to trust God's gracious plan to unite all things in Christ. Trust God's

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:33.510
gracious plan to unite all things in Christ. And we're just taking short passages in Ephesians as

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:37.490
we go through it this year. So today we're going to go through verses 7 through 10. I'll go ahead

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:44.370
and read this for us. It says this, in him we have redemption through his blood, the forgiveness of

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:50.190
our trespasses, according to the riches of his grace, which he lavished upon us in all wisdom

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:56.170
and insight, making known to us the mystery of his will, according to his purpose, which he set

00:08:56.170 --> 00:09:02.070
forth in Christ as a plan for the fullness of time, to unite all things in Christ, things in

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:07.870
heaven and things on earth in him. Amen. Let me pray for us as we've read God's word. Lord, we invite

00:09:07.870 --> 00:09:13.710
you once again into this time, into this spiritual family that you have brought together and formed

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:19.190
for your purposes. We invite you here, Lord, because we want to hear from you. We want you to speak to

00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:25.350
us through your holy word, Lord, to give us hope, to give us direction, to show us the power of the

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:32.810
gospel message, to lead us in living in that gospel truth and power, to be a testimony of your

00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:38.390
saving grace, Lord, and to live in the joy of that truth together. God, we pray that you come and do

00:09:38.390 --> 00:09:43.330
this work in each and every one of us today, God, and in us as a community together. Lead us in your

00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:47.130
word and in the life you've called us to, Lord. Lead us into that higher calling that you have

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:53.310
for each one of us. In Jesus' name we pray. Amen. Amen. So as we look into today's passage, many of

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:58.030
you probably remember, if you were with us last week, we looked at verses 3 through 6 in Ephesians

00:09:58.030 --> 00:10:03.570
1, and we heard how the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ has blessed us in Christ with

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:09.430
every spiritual blessing in the heavenly places. And we started to unpack a little bit what those

00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:14.010
spiritual blessings were. But we know it's going to go on through the rest of Ephesians 1. We saw

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:19.770
some of those spiritual blessings, like being chosen as holy and blameless children of God that

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:25.670
were adopted as God's daughters and sons, as children of the King. And today, we're going to

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:30.830
see more of those blessings unfold and be invited to live in those together and kind of see the

00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:35.610
ultimate purpose behind those blessings as well. So as we start to dig into this passage, what we

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:41.950
first want to focus in on are some really powerful words like redemption, forgiveness, and grace.

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:47.950
And these are like pretty common Christian terms. If you hang out with Christians or in the church

00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:52.610
long enough, you'll probably hear these words a lot of different ways. People kind of use them

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:57.150
very frequently among Christians. For some of you who may be new to the church, and I want to

00:10:57.150 --> 00:11:01.810
welcome you if you are new to the church, if you're new, checking out the Christian faith, exploring,

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:06.270
asking questions. Maybe a friend invited you. We're so glad you're here. We want to make sure

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:10.390
everyone's on the same page of what these mean. But also, even for those of us who are Christians,

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:14.910
we may sometimes throw these terms around without really digging in and knowing what it is that

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:19.110
we're saying when we use these terms. And so we want to, all of us, get on the same page with

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:23.850
that here today. And so let's think about redemption and forgiveness to start. I'll give you just

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:27.890
Greek background of these words real quick. The word translated as redemption, that's

00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:35.050
apolitrosis. It means to release or set free with the implied analogy to the process of freeing a

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:40.810
slave. So to set free, to liberate, to deliver, to redeem. So this is specifically talking about

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:46.650
buying someone out of slavery. That's what redemption means. That's what it means to

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:51.270
redeem. We're not talking about a general idea. We're not talking about maybe like a football team

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:56.190
trying to redeem themselves or something. We're talking about redeeming out of slavery. That's

00:11:56.190 --> 00:12:01.170
what this means. Then if we look at forgiveness, what's that? Where's that come from? It's a Greek

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:07.950
word, aphesis. It means to remove the guilt resulting from wrongdoing. So if somebody is

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:13.130
guilty because of something they've done wrong, aphesis, forgiveness, that means to remove

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:18.670
that guilt somehow. So when we look into this passage and we're trying to understand what it

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:25.490
is talking about, it's talking about that this is what Jesus has done for us. This is what happens

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:30.930
when we are in Christ, and as we look at this here, we're not only seeing what Jesus has done

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:37.610
for us, we also get to see the other side of the coin of what sin actually does in our lives,

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:42.130
and so I want us to dig in there a little bit. As much as it's not fun to talk about sin,

00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:46.770
it's important because if we don't talk about the reality of sin, we're not going to really

00:12:46.770 --> 00:12:51.790
understand and appreciate what it is that Jesus has done for us, so specifically, if we just take

00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:56.690
the flip side of this coin, we can understand in this passage that what sin does is it makes us

00:12:56.690 --> 00:13:04.070
guilty and enslaved. That's what sin does. And let's dig into that a little bit. Like when I say

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:10.410
sin makes us guilty and enslaved, I'm referring to the spiritual effect of sin on our lives.

00:13:10.590 --> 00:13:15.210
I also don't want us to forget that we're not merely victims of sin, but we're perpetrators

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:19.790
of sin. Sin is not just something we're victims of and we're just kind of passively like, oh,

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:26.350
sin happens to me. No, we sin. We do things that are against God's will. We rebel against his

00:13:26.350 --> 00:13:31.310
revealed word, his command. We do things that offend his honor and glory. We do things that

00:13:31.310 --> 00:13:37.890
sin against one another as well. We all do that. The effect of that on our lives is that we become

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:43.410
guilty and enslaved. And so think about this here. Paul, as he's writing this letter to the church in

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:49.070
Ephesus, he's not coming up with these as like new ideas out of nowhere. He, in all of this, he's

00:13:49.070 --> 00:13:54.550
referring back to the Old Testament that the Jewish people had known for centuries at this

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:58.830
point. And he's thinking probably about things like Proverbs 5.22. It says,

00:13:58.990 --> 00:14:05.390
the iniquities of the wicked ensnare him, and he is held fast in the cords of sin.

00:14:06.050 --> 00:14:10.770
So he's saying when we fall into sin, we actually become ensnared by it, trapped by it,

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:14.650
unable to get out of it. If I could describe what that might actually look like just in a

00:14:14.650 --> 00:14:22.090
very simple example. Like, just imagine that a person has been dishonest about a seemingly small

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:27.130
matter. Maybe they told what they thought was a little white lie to get out of the consequences

00:14:27.130 --> 00:14:33.290
of something, okay? So, maybe like, oh, did you, you know, maybe something like, did you leave the

00:14:33.290 --> 00:14:39.270
toilet seat up? No, I didn't do that. Somebody else must have come along and done that. Okay,

00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:43.770
well, it seems like a little white lie to get out of being in trouble for not putting the toilet

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:50.230
the seat down. It seems small. But now imagine, somebody else comes along and says, well, no one

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:57.250
else has gone in and out of that bathroom. Maybe now that person's kind of stuck. Now, okay, I need

00:14:57.250 --> 00:15:02.290
to come up with another lie to cover up my previous lie if I don't want to take responsibility for it.

00:15:02.870 --> 00:15:06.850
And so I come up with something of like, oh, maybe the dog went in there and did it.

00:15:08.050 --> 00:15:11.310
And then someone says, oh, but the dog's been outside for the last two hours. Oh,

00:15:11.310 --> 00:15:20.270
God. Maybe the cat. I don't know. You see, people start coming up with lies to maintain lies. And

00:15:20.270 --> 00:15:27.030
the further you go down that path, now significant relationships are on the line. And a person's

00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:33.030
reputation in front of others are on the line. And this becomes a snare wrapped around a person

00:15:33.030 --> 00:15:40.130
that started with just a small seed of seemingly meaningless dishonesty that now has ensnared that

00:15:40.130 --> 00:15:46.010
person, where now they have to keep that lie going and try to use deception to avoid responsibility

00:15:46.010 --> 00:15:53.690
and save face, and that becomes habitual. It becomes the way we live. And you can apply that

00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:59.230
to anything, right? I'm just using a very small, simple example. How much more powerful is that

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:07.350
snare, that enslavement, when it deals with things that are of heavier weight and their impact on our

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:14.170
lives and on those around us. And I want us to realize here as we think about this, that this

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:21.290
starts affecting us in a lot of different ways. And this ends up damaging the relationship with

00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:28.070
people around us, but especially damaging our relationship with God. That's the biggest deal

00:16:28.070 --> 00:16:34.270
here. We tend to think and be more aware of how sin affects relationship with people, when somebody's

00:16:34.270 --> 00:16:39.790
offended, when trust is broken, things like that. But this most deeply affects our relationship with

00:16:39.790 --> 00:16:43.630
God. And here again, Paul, he's not coming up with these days out of nowhere. He's probably

00:16:43.630 --> 00:16:48.450
thinking way back to the beginning in a creation account. Go back to Genesis chapter 2 when what

00:16:48.450 --> 00:16:52.750
does God tell Adam? It says, the Lord God commanded the man saying, you may surely eat of

00:16:52.750 --> 00:16:57.230
every tree of the garden, but of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil you shall not eat,

00:16:57.230 --> 00:17:00.550
For in the day that you eat of it, you shall surely die.

00:17:02.330 --> 00:17:08.510
From the beginning, God said, hey, if you do this thing I'm telling you not to do,

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:10.410
it's not a small matter.

00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:12.590
It's going to lead to death.

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:15.950
And here, really think about that.

00:17:16.090 --> 00:17:21.490
How easily we fall into this and the destruction that brings in our relationship with God.

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:24.470
And let me show you this painting real quick.

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:26.190
This is my favorite painting of the Garden of Eden.

00:17:27.070 --> 00:17:28.850
And I've probably shared it with our church before.

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:29.770
It's by Thomas Cole.

00:17:30.670 --> 00:17:36.130
And I love this painting because in most depictions of the Garden of Eden, what do you see?

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:41.990
In most depictions of the Garden of Eden, you see like the tree is right there with the fruit on it.

00:17:42.430 --> 00:17:45.750
And Adam and Eve, they're just hanging out there in all their glory, right?

00:17:45.890 --> 00:17:47.170
And then there's the serpent.

